<ol class="breathe-horizontal" start="1"> 


  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17935">arXiv:2610.17935</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.17935">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2610.17935">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2610.17935">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Cryptography and Security">cs.CR</span>
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Provenance and Authorization in Autobiographical <span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">Models</span>
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Alpay%2C+F">Faruk Alpay</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Alpay%2C+T">Taylan Alpay</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.17935v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Autobiographical <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span>&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.17935v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.17935v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.17935v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Autobiographical <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> may draw on archives containing observations, quotations, emotional reports, and earlier model outputs. Cryptographic authentication can preserve an archive while leaving these sources semantically conflated. We formulate this gap as a computer-security problem: narrative content must not silently become consent or alter an authorized decision rule. We define typed records, authenticated attestations, a separate policy channel, and admissible views. Under explicit assumptions, we give a standard integrity reduction and a <span class="search-hit mathjax">noninterference</span> argument, characterize the information required by exact decision-preserving summaries, and show why passive history and fixed-point convergence need not identify a preferred action. Alpay Algebra supplies a comparative language for iterative memory without being a premise of the proofs. Sociology of religious affiliation, source-monitoring psychology, and self-determination theory motivate distinctions in a bounded Latter-day Saint vignette. A separate, reproducible pilot uses a frozen public <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">model</span> on 32 wholly synthetic cases across 576 paired conditions. It compares flattened records, source labels, and an explicit admission gate under contradictory summary repetition. The study concerns computational attribution and authorization, not the motives or psychology of the people in the vignette.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.17935v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.17935v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 13 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="comments is-size-7">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Comments:</span>
      <span class="has-text-grey-dark mathjax">19 pages, 1 table, 0 figures in the manuscript. Includes appendices. Reproducibility code, synthetic data, recorded model outputs, and supplementary plots are provided as ancillary files</span>
    </p>
    

    
      <p class="comments is-size-7">
        

        
          <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">MSC Class:</span>
          68M25; 68T50; 68P30
        

        
          <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">ACM Class:</span>
          D.4.6; I.2.7
        
      </p>
    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15883">arXiv:2610.15883</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.15883">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2610.15883">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2610.15883">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Cryptography and Security">cs.CR</span>
        
          
            <span class="tag is-small is-grey tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Artificial Intelligence">cs.AI</span>
          
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        What the Model Cannot See: Covert and Provable Text Invisibility in LLMs
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Russinovich%2C+M">Mark Russinovich</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.15883v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Text wrapped in a trigger can be made behaviorally invisible to a <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span>&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.15883v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.15883v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.15883v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Text wrapped in a trigger can be made behaviorally invisible to a <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">model</span>: omitted from summaries and quotations, ignored in question answering, and left unflagged by downstream filtering. We study this selective blindness as a dual-use information-flow primitive, a supply-chain backdoor, and a white-box auditing problem. We first implement it exactly as a serving-time span veto: blocking information flow out of a marked span makes every outside activation bit-identical under same-length substitution of the span&#39;s content, a <span class="search-hit mathjax">noninterference</span> property we verify across ten models, seven mixer families, and 0.5B-120B parameters. The veto serves as a deployable quarantine, an exact reference for calibrating leakage, and a teacher. Distilled into weights, it yields a backdoor: checkpoints trained on six model families from 4B to 120B ignore marked context at little benchmark cost, and their outputs show no measurable semantic trace of the suppressed content above the veto reference on our held-out suite; an in-domain variant suppresses planted vulnerabilities during AI code review. Detection is therefore a white-box problem. Simpler backdoors remain internally transparent -- linear probes recover suppressed content at 0.57-0.79 accuracy versus 0.025 chance -- but hardening the attack until probes reach chance collapses attention from outside the span into it below the range of base models. Across the attacks we evaluate, probe visibility trades off against routing visibility; we do not establish that this tradeoff is fundamental.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.15883v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.15883v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 11 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09012">arXiv:2610.09012</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.09012">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2610.09012">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2610.09012">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Cryptography and Security">cs.CR</span>
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        TwinGuard-Lite: A Rule-Based State-Admission Gateway for Generative Patient Digital Twins
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Chu%2C+W">Wenhui Chu</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Islam%2C+S+R">Sheikh Rabiul Islam</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.09012v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Future generative patient digital twins may combine longitudinal health records with <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span>-&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.09012v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2610.09012v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2610.09012v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Future generative patient digital twins may combine longitudinal health records with <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span>-<span class="search-hit mathjax">model</span> agents and keep information across sessions. The wording of a proposed update does not reveal whether it comes from an allowed source, conflicts with the patient&#39;s record, or belongs to someone else. We present TwinGuard-Lite, a rule-based gateway that admits an update to a twin&#39;s persistent state only if it passes checkable approximations of two integrity properties. Grounded state consistency (GSC) is approximated by a provenance check and a contradiction check; cross-patient <span class="search-hit mathjax">noninterference</span> (CPN) is approximated by a namespace check against trusted transport metadata. We evaluate three attack families over 20 seeded person-level splits of a semi-synthetic stream built from the openly licensed eICU database demo, with 29,131 $\pm$ 2706 candidate updates per seed. A keyword filter and an anomaly detector detect 16% and 24% of attacks, respectively; a provenance-only ablation detects 59.9% but misses every cross-patient attack. Combining GSC and CPN yields 99.1% precision, 90.5% recall, a 94.6% F1 score, and a 0.033% false-positive rate. Every attack the full gateway admits falsely claims a trusted source; when the retrieval channel does so, 92.9% of retrieval attacks are admitted. The results hold under stated trust assumptions, and no <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">model</span>, agent, or retriever is executed: TwinGuard-Lite is a mechanism-level proof of concept that motivates authenticated, patient-bound ingestion, not a clinical safeguard.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2610.09012v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2610.09012v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 6 October, 2026; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> October 2026.
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="comments is-size-7">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Comments:</span>
      <span class="has-text-grey-dark mathjax">6 pages, 2 figures, 1 table. Accepted to HealthSec&#39;26 (Cybersecurity in Healthcare), co-located with ACSAC 2026</span>
    </p>
    

    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20064">arXiv:2602.20064</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.20064">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2602.20064">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2602.20064">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Programming Languages">cs.PL</span>
        
          
            <span class="tag is-small is-grey tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Artificial Intelligence">cs.AI</span>
          
            <span class="tag is-small is-grey tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Cryptography and Security">cs.CR</span>
          
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        The LLMbda Calculus: AI Agents, Conversations, and Information Flow
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Garby%2C+Z">Zac Garby</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Gordon%2C+A+D">Andrew D. Gordon</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Sands%2C+D">David Sands</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2602.20064v2-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Large <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span>&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2602.20064v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2602.20064v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2602.20064v2-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Large <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> are increasingly deployed as agents: they plan, call tools, read untrusted data, and act on the results. This exposes them to prompt injection: data meant only to be read is obeyed as an instruction. The most principled defences replace content inspection with provenance: classifying data by source and keeping trusted and untrusted apart through a separation of duty (the dual-LLM pattern) and information-flow control. Yet the leading systems are hard to fully trust: flow tracking is easy to get wrong, deliberate relaxations are hard to audit, and the dual-LLM pattern is hard-wired into the architecture. We present LLMbda, an untyped call-by-value lambda calculus that makes provenance-based defence both expressible and provably sound, without committing to an architecture. It adds the operational core of agentic systems as first-class constructs: prompt-response conversations that can be forked and cleared, code generation, and dynamic information-flow control in which every value carries a label that every reduction propagates. Isolation becomes a policy a program expresses, and reclassification an explicit, auditable construct. Our central result is a termination-insensitive probabilistic <span class="search-hit mathjax">noninterference</span> theorem over the whole calculus, including code-generating agents, with an insulated variant that holds even when the attacker chooses all untrusted inputs. The verified interpreter is itself the harness that calls the model, to our knowledge the first LLM agent harness whose executable is the subject of machine-checked security theorems, so every agent inherits the guarantee. On the AgentDojo banking benchmark, an agent built within LLMbda, enforcement always on, matches the utility of CaMeL, a leading dual-LLM defence, run without its policy checks (which halve its utility), and resists all but two of 1296 attacked runs. Our harness and all proofs are in Lean.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2602.20064v2-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2602.20064v2-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 10 July, 2026; <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">v1</span> submitted 23 February, 2026;
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> February 2026.
      
    </p>
    

    
      <p class="comments is-size-7">
        

        

        
          <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">ACM Class:</span>
          D.3.1; I.2.5
        
      </p>
    

    
  </li>

  <li class="arxiv-result">
    <div class="is-marginless">
      <p class="list-title is-inline-block"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.16504">arXiv:2407.16504</a>
        <span>&nbsp;[<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.16504">pdf</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/ps/2407.16504">ps</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/format/2407.16504">other</a>]&nbsp;</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tags is-inline-block">
        <span class="tag is-small is-link tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Cryptography and Security">cs.CR</span>
        
          
            <span class="tag is-small is-grey tooltip is-tooltip-top" data-tooltip="Programming Languages">cs.PL</span>
          
        </div>
      
    </div>
    
    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        Language-Based Security for Low-Level MPC
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Skalka%2C+C">Christian Skalka</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Near%2C+J+P">Joseph P. Near</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2407.16504v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        &hellip;a new staged PL for defining a variety of low-level probabilistic MPC protocols. We also formulate a collection of confidentiality and integrity hyperproperties for our <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">model</span> that are familiar from information flow, including conditional <span class="search-hit mathjax">noninterference</span>, gradual release,&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2407.16504v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2407.16504v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2407.16504v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Secure Multi-Party Computation (MPC) is an important enabling technology for data privacy in modern distributed applications. Currently, proof methods for low-level MPC protocols are primarily manual and thus tedious and error-prone, and are also non-standardized and unfamiliar to most PL theorists. As a step towards better language support and language-based enforcement, we develop a new staged PL for defining a variety of low-level probabilistic MPC protocols. We also formulate a collection of confidentiality and integrity hyperproperties for our <span class="search-hit mathjax">language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">model</span> that are familiar from information flow, including conditional <span class="search-hit mathjax">noninterference</span>, gradual release, and robust declassification. We demonstrate their relation to standard MPC threat models of passive and malicious security, and how they can be leveraged in security verification of protocols. To prove these properties we develop automated tactics in $\mathbb{F}_2$ that can be integrated with separation logic-style reasoning.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2407.16504v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2407.16504v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 23 July, 2024; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> July 2024.
      
    </p>
    

    

    
